"# Hooks on one agent\n\nUse aos-agent hook to run a program when something happens to one agent. It writes only that agent's own `<stack>/hooks.json`; the definition's `hooks.json` stays as it is. The definition must declare `{\"aos-agent\": {\"hooks\": \"hooks.json\"}}` in `interfaces.json`, otherwise `HooksMissing`.\n\n```sh\naos-agent hook -t bob                       # = ls: NAME TAB EVENT TAB HANDLER\naos-agent hook -t bob ls -l                 # also CONDITION ASYNC ON_ERROR INTERCEPT TIMEOUT_MS FROM, and hooks set to null\naos-agent hook -t bob ls --json\naos-agent hook -t bob get guard\naos-agent hook -t bob add --event before_tool --condition agents_ctl hooks/guard    # its answer counts (intercept is on by default; --no-intercept turns it off)\naos-agent hook -t bob add --event brain_failed --on-error continue --async -n ping --path-json '{\"$env\": \"AOS_HOME\", \"$path\": \"/lib/x\"}'\naos-agent hook -t bob rm guard              # a definition hook is set to null; an own hook is deleted\naos-agent hook -t bob reset -y              # delete the agent's own list, back to the definition's\n```\n\n- The name defaults to the file or folder name; with `--path-json`, `--name` is required. `NameBad`: empty, has `/`, or starts with `.`.\n- A relative path is from the current directory; missing = `PathMissing`.\n- `HookExists`: add `--replace`. `HookMissing`: no hook of that name. `HooksBad`: the list is broken.\n- Events, the answer protocol and options are the same as for the agent's own tool: `aos/me.json#/self_hook`."
